# certdoctor - .gitignore
#
# NOTE ON REFERENCES: this file used to point at the planning documents by
# number - "see docs/16" and so on. Those documents are the ones this very
# file keeps out of the repository, so every such pointer was an instruction
# to read something the reader cannot have. Reasons are stated here instead.
#
# WARNING: this file is what keeps the Polish planning documents out of the public
# repository. Removing an entry from the "planning documents" block below does not
# just add files to the repo - it publishes internal notes.

# --- Planning documents. Polish, internal, never published. ---
/docs/
/CLAUDE.md
/PLAN.md
/BACKLOG.md

# The technical changelog joined the block above on 2026-08-02, by the owner's
# decision. It stays in English despite now sitting outside the repository -
# the place criterion permits the owner's language, it does not demand it, and
# this file is half test names and type names either way.
#
# It was tracked before that date, so it remains in the history and can be read
# there. That was known and accepted.
#
# Consequence worth knowing before removing this line: a public clone now has no
# record of what was measured and why, beyond the comments in the code. Putting
# the line back does not merely add a file - it publishes the whole measurement
# log at once.
/CHANGELOG-DEV.md

# --- Development tooling and measurement rigs. Never shipped, never a CI dependency. ---
# Not to be confused with /tools/, which IS tracked and IS a CI dependency.
/internal_tools/

# --- Build output ---
# WARNING: these two names must stay anchored to the repository root with a
# leading slash. Unanchored, "certdoctor" also matches the directory
# cmd/certdoctor/, so the program's entry point silently never gets committed
# and a fresh clone has no main package to build. Caught by git check-ignore on
# 2026-08-02, after the pattern had already been in place.
/dist/
/build/
/certdoctor
/certdoctor.exe
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# --- Test and coverage artifacts ---
#
# ANCHORED TO THE ROOT, and that is the point rather than tidiness. Unanchored,
# "*.out" also matches a golden file called help.out in any testdata directory -
# and the guards' golden data vanishing is precisely the failure the warning at
# the bottom of this file describes. This repository has already paid for that
# lesson once, with a pattern that excluded cmd/certdoctor/.
#
# "*.test" stays unanchored on purpose: it is the name the Go toolchain gives a
# compiled test binary, in whichever package directory it was built.
*.test
/*.out
/coverage.*
/testbed/**/generated/

# Byte-exact backups the mutation register makes beside the file it is editing.
# They are removed when the run finishes - this line is for the run that does
# NOT finish, because an interrupted one leaves a copy of a production file
# sitting next to it and "git add -A" would commit it.
*.mutbak

# --- Testbed material generated at startup, NEVER committed ---
# Private keys and certificates for the fake corporate CA are produced when the
# testbed starts. Committing a private key trips secret scanners and is permanent
# in git history.
/testbed/**/*.key
/testbed/**/*.pem
/testbed/**/*.p12
/testbed/**/*.pfx
/testbed/**/*.jks
/testbed/**/*.crt
/testbed/**/*.cer

# WARNING - do NOT add a blanket pattern for "anything that looks generated".
# The testbed's expected verdicts are the guards' golden data. They MUST stay in
# the repository. Excluding them turns every end-to-end guard green with nothing
# left to check against.
!/testbed/**/expected/**

# --- Local editor and OS state ---
.claude/settings.local.json
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Local environment ---
.env
*.local
